package com.nulljosh.nimble

/**
 * BC First Peoples 12 with no network: the diversity of First Peoples in BC, oral tradition, colonization and the
 * Indian Act, residential schools, title cases, treaties, resistance and reconciliation. A port of `fp12()` in
 * docs/engine.js; the tables are copied from there by script.
 */
internal object FirstPeoples12 {
    /** Phrase questions, matched anywhere. */
    private val facts = listOf(
        """\bunceded\b""" to "Land that was never given up by treaty or sale. Most of BC is unceded: apart from the Douglas Treaties, Treaty 8 and a few modern treaties, First Nations never signed away their lands, including where Vancouver and Victoria stand.",
        """\b(?:importance|role|significance) of (?:the )?land\b|\bland\b.*\b(?:important|importance)\b.*\b(?:first peoples|first nations|indigenous)\b""" to "For First Peoples the land is a relative, not a commodity: it holds identity, language, law, food, medicine and the ancestors, and brings responsibilities to care for it for the generations to come. Each nation's stories and place names tie it to its territory.",
        """\b(?:4|four) rs?\b.*\breconciliation\b|\breconciliation\b.*\b(?:4|four) rs?\b|\b(?:4|four) rs of\b""" to "Respect, Relevance, Reciprocity and Responsibility, from Verna Kirkness and Ray Barnhardt's 1991 paper on First Nations in higher education, now widely used to frame reconciliation in education.",
        """\bsmallpox\b.*\b(?:bc|british columbia|1862|first nations|epidemic)\b""" to "The 1862 smallpox epidemic began in Victoria and spread up the coast and into the Interior after colonial officials forced First Nations camps out of the town. It killed an estimated third or more of the First Nations people in BC, more in some nations, and opened land to settlers.",
        """\blanguage famil(?:y|ies)\b.*\b(?:first nations|indigenous|bc|british columbia)\b|\b(?:first nations|indigenous)\b.*\blanguage famil(?:y|ies)\b""" to "The largest are Salishan (such as Halq'eméylem and Secwepemctsín), Wakashan (Kwak'wala and Nuu-chah-nulth), Dene or Athabaskan (Dakelh and Tsilhqot'in) and Tsimshianic (Sm'algyax, Nisga'a and Gitxsan). Haida and Ktunaxa are isolates, related to no other language, and Tlingit and Cree are also spoken.",
        """\b(?:importance|role|significance) of (?:the )?(?:western red )?cedar\b|\bcedar\b.*\b(?:important|importance|significan)""" to "Coast peoples call the western red cedar the tree of life. Its wood made plank houses, canoes, totem poles, boxes and tools, its bark was woven into clothing, hats, baskets and mats, and its roots into baskets. Trees are often harvested with ceremony and thanks, and bark is stripped so the tree lives.",
        """\b(?:importance|role|significance) of (?:the )?salmon\b|\bsalmon'?s? (?:importance|role|significance)\b""" to "Salmon has been the heart of life for most BC First Nations for thousands of years: the main food, dried and smoked for winter, a basis of trade and wealth, and central to ceremonies, stories and law about respect and sharing. Fishing rights are protected by section 35 and were confirmed in cases like Sparrow.",
        """\b(?:first nations|indigenous|status indians?)\b.*\b(?:right to vote|vote|franchise)\b.*\b(?:bc|british columbia)\b|\b(?:bc|british columbia)\b.*\b(?:first nations|indigenous)\b.*\bvote\b""" to "1949 for BC provincial elections. Frank Calder of the Nisga'a was elected to the BC legislature that same year. First Nations got the federal vote, without losing status, in 1960.",
        """\b1927\b.*\b(?:land claims?|lawyers?|indian act)\b|\b(?:ban|banned)\b.*\bland claims?\b""" to "A 1927 Indian Act amendment, aimed at the Allied Tribes of BC who were pressing land claims, made it illegal to raise money or hire a lawyer to pursue a claim against the government without permission. It stopped land claims for decades and was repealed in 1951.",
        """\bkamloops\b.*\b(?:residential school|graves|215|discovery)\b|\btk'?eml(?:u|ú)ps\b.*\b(?:graves|discovery|215)\b""" to "In May 2021 the Tk'emlúps te Secwépemc announced that ground-penetrating radar had found about 215 probable unmarked graves of children at the former Kamloops Indian Residential School, one of the largest in Canada. It led to searches at other school sites across the country.",
        """\bstatement of apology\b|\bapology\b.*\b(?:2008|residential schools?)\b|\b(?:2008|harper)\b.*\bapology\b""" to "On June 11, 2008, Prime Minister Stephen Harper apologized in the House of Commons, on behalf of the Government of Canada, for the residential school system, saying \"we are sorry\" for its purpose of killing \"the Indian in the child\", with survivors in the gallery.",
        """\brole of (?:the )?elders\b|\belders'? role\b""" to "Elders are respected knowledge keepers who carry the language, history, laws, ceremonies and teachings of their nation and pass them on, guide decisions and help settle disputes. Being an Elder comes from wisdom and the community's respect, not age alone.",
        """^(?!.*\blanguages?\b).*\bhow many first nations\b.*\b(?:bc|british columbia)\b""" to "About 204, more than any other province: about a third of all First Nations in Canada are in BC.",
        """\bhow many\b.*\b(?:first nations|indigenous|aboriginal) languages?\b.*\b(?:bc|british columbia)\b|\bhow many languages\b.*\bfirst nations\b.*\b(?:bc|british columbia)\b""" to "34 First Nations languages, with about 90 dialects, in seven language families: about 60 percent of the First Nations languages in Canada. Most are critically endangered, and communities are working to revitalize them.",
        """\bpotlatch\b.*\b(?:ban|banned|outlaw|prohibit)\w*\b.*\b(?:lifted|end|ended|repealed|removed)\b|\b(?:lifted|end|ended|repealed|removed)\b.*\bpotlatch\b""" to "1951, when the Indian Act was revised and the ban was quietly dropped, after 67 years.",
        """\bpotlatch\b.*\b(?:ban|banned|outlaw|prohibit)\w*\b|\b(?:ban|banned|outlaw)\w*\b.*\bpotlatch\b""" to "1884, by an amendment to the Indian Act that took effect on January 1, 1885, making it a crime to hold or take part in a potlatch, along with other ceremonies. Many people were jailed and ceremonial regalia were seized, as after Dan Cranmer's potlatch in 1921. It was lifted in 1951.",
        """\bhow many calls to action\b|\bnumber of calls to action\b""" to "94. The Truth and Reconciliation Commission issued its 94 Calls to Action in June 2015, covering child welfare, education, language and culture, health, justice and reconciliation.",
    ).map { Regex(it.first) to it.second }

    /** Peoples, people, places, events and ideas, matched as the whole term by Terms. */
    private val terms = listOf(
        """(?:a )?potlatch(?:es)?""" to "A ceremony and feast of the Northwest Coast First Nations, such as the Kwakwaka'wakw, Haida and Nuu-chah-nulth, held to mark births, names, marriages, deaths and the passing of rights. The host gives away gifts, and the witnesses confirm the names, rights and history recorded there, so it works as the people's government, law and economy. Canada banned it from 1885 to 1951.",
        """(?:indian |first nations )?status|(?:a )?status indians?|(?:a )?registered indians?""" to "Being registered as an \"Indian\" under the federal Indian Act, which gives certain rights and benefits, such as living on reserve and some tax exemptions. The government decides who qualifies, which for a long time stripped status from First Nations women who married non-status men.",
        """(?:an? )?(?:indian |first nations )?reserves?""" to "In Canada, a reserve is land set aside under the Indian Act for the use of a First Nation band, with the title held by the Crown. BC has about 1,500 reserves, mostly small, laid out in the 1800s and early 1900s.",
        """(?:a )?band councils?|(?:a )?first nations? band councils?""" to "The elected government of a First Nation band set up under the Indian Act, with a chief and councillors who run local services on reserve. In many nations it sits alongside a traditional hereditary system.",
        """(?:the )?national day for truth and reconciliation""" to "September 30, a federal statutory holiday since 2021, honouring the children who never came home from residential schools, the survivors, and their families and communities. It is also Orange Shirt Day.",
        """orange shirt day""" to "September 30, begun in 2013 in Williams Lake, BC, after Phyllis Webstad told how her new orange shirt was taken from her on her first day at St. Joseph's Mission residential school in 1973. Its message is \"Every Child Matters.\"",
        """(?:the )?douglas treaties|(?:the )?fort victoria treaties""" to "Fourteen land purchases made between 1850 and 1854 by James Douglas of the Hudson's Bay Company with First Nations on Vancouver Island, around Victoria, Saanich, Sooke, Nanaimo and Fort Rupert. The written versions say the land was surrendered; the First Nations understood them as peace agreements keeping their villages, fields and the right to hunt and fish.",
        """treaty (?:8|eight)""" to "A numbered treaty signed in 1899 with First Nations of northern Alberta, northeastern BC, northwestern Saskatchewan and the southern Northwest Territories, as gold seekers poured north to the Klondike. It is the only numbered treaty in BC, covering the Peace River region.",
        """(?:the )?tsilhqot'?in (?:case|decision|ruling)|tsilhqot'?in nation v\.? british columbia|(?:the )?tsilhqot'?in""" to "Tsilhqot'in Nation v British Columbia (2014): the Supreme Court of Canada recognized Aboriginal title to about 1,750 square kilometres of the Tsilhqot'in Nation's land west of Williams Lake, the first time a court declared title to specific land. It confirmed that title covers land used regularly and exclusively, not just village sites.",
        """(?:the )?sparrow (?:case|decision)|r\.? v\.? sparrow""" to "R v Sparrow (1990): Ronald Sparrow of the Musqueam Nation was charged for fishing with a net longer than allowed. The Supreme Court ruled that Aboriginal rights protected by section 35 can be limited only for a justified reason, with priority for food fishing, setting the Sparrow test.",
        """(?:the )?red paper|citizens plus""" to "\"Citizens Plus\", the 1970 response to the federal White Paper by the Indian Association of Alberta, led by Harold Cardinal. It rejected assimilation and argued that First Nations hold their own rights and treaties on top of citizenship.",
        """(?:indigenous |aboriginal |first nations )?self[- ]government""" to "The right of Indigenous nations to govern themselves, making their own laws and running their own lands, services and institutions, replacing rule under the Indian Act. In BC it has come through treaties, such as the Nisga'a Final Agreement, and other agreements.",
        """dripa|(?:the )?declaration on the rights of indigenous peoples act""" to "BC's Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples Act, passed unanimously in November 2019, the first law in Canada to adopt the UN Declaration (UNDRIP). It commits the province to bring its laws into line with it, with an action plan.",
        """(?:the )?mckenna[- ]mcbride (?:royal )?commission|(?:the )?royal commission on indian affairs for (?:the province of )?british columbia""" to "The federal and BC royal commission from 1913 to 1916 that reviewed BC's reserves. It cut about 36,000 acres of valuable land from reserves (the \"cut-off lands\") and added poorer land, without First Nations' consent, which led to long-running claims.",
        """(?:the )?haida(?: nation| people)?""" to "The First Nation of Haida Gwaii, the islands off BC's north coast, known for their great canoes, carved poles and art, such as the work of Bill Reid. The Council of the Haida Nation governs, and BC recognized Haida title to Haida Gwaii in 2024.",
        """(?:the )?coast salish(?: peoples?)?""" to "The peoples of the southern BC coast and Puget Sound, speaking Salishan languages, including the Musqueam, Squamish, Tsleil-Waututh, Stó:lō, Tsawwassen, Cowichan and Songhees. Vancouver sits on unceded Musqueam, Squamish and Tsleil-Waututh land.",
        """(?:a )?totem poles?""" to "A monumental pole carved from western red cedar by Northwest Coast First Nations, showing crests (animals and beings) that record a family's lineage, history and rights. Poles are raised to honour the dead, mark a house or tell a story, usually at a potlatch.",
        """(?:a )?hereditary chiefs?""" to "A leader whose title is passed down through a family, house or clan, often through the mother's line, under a nation's own laws, not the Indian Act. Among the Wet'suwet'en, Gitxsan and others, hereditary chiefs hold authority over the house territories.",
        """(?:a )?matrilineal(?: society| system| descent)?|matrilineality""" to "A system in which descent, names, clan membership and inheritance pass through the mother's line. Many BC First Nations, such as the Haida, Tlingit, Tsimshian, Gitxsan and Nisga'a, are matrilineal.",
        """idle no more""" to "A grassroots Indigenous rights movement started in November 2012 by four women in Saskatchewan, Nina Wilson, Sheelah McLean, Sylvia McAdam and Jessica Gordon, in protest against Bill C-45's weakening of water and land protections. It spread through round dances, rallies and teach-ins across Canada.",
        """(?:the )?mmiwg(?:2s)?(?: inquiry)?|(?:the )?national inquiry into missing and murdered indigenous women and girls""" to "The National Inquiry into Missing and Murdered Indigenous Women and Girls, from 2016 to 2019. Its final report, Reclaiming Power and Place, found the violence amounted to a genocide and made 231 Calls for Justice.",
        """cultural appropriation""" to "Taking or using the symbols, art, dress, stories or ceremonies of another culture, especially a marginalized one, without permission, understanding or credit, such as selling fake \"Native\" art or wearing a headdress as a costume.",
        """(?:a )?longhouses?|(?:a )?plank houses?""" to "A large house built of cedar planks over a cedar post-and-beam frame by Northwest Coast First Nations, home to several related families of one house group. Coast Salish longhouses are still used for winter ceremonies and gatherings.",
        """(?:the )?first salmon ceremony""" to "A ceremony held when the first salmon of the season is caught: it is welcomed, cooked and shared with the community, and its bones are returned to the river so the salmon will come back, showing respect for the salmon people.",
        """enfranchisement""" to "Under the Indian Act, the loss of Indian status in exchange for the vote and full citizenship. It was voluntary at first, then forced on First Nations people who earned a university degree, became doctors, lawyers or clergy, served in the military, or were women who married non-status men, until 1985.",
        """(?:the )?pass system""" to "A policy from 1885, after the North-West Resistance, that required First Nations people on the Prairies to get a written pass from the Indian agent to leave their reserve. It was never law but was enforced for decades.",
        """(?:an? )?indian agents?""" to "A federal official who ran Indian Act policy on reserves, with wide powers over daily life: deciding who could leave, controlling money and farming, enforcing the potlatch ban and sending children to residential schools.",
        """bill c-?31""" to "The 1985 amendment to the Indian Act that ended the rule stripping status from First Nations women who married non-status men, and let them and their children regain status. It also let bands control their own membership.",
        """intergenerational trauma""" to "Trauma passed down from people who lived through it, such as residential school survivors, to their children and grandchildren, through its effects on parenting, family, health, language and identity.",
        """(?:the )?(?:bc |british columbia )?treaty (?:process|commission)|(?:the )?bc treaty commission""" to "The modern treaty process set up by Canada, BC and the First Nations Summit, with the BC Treaty Commission created in 1992 and talks beginning in 1993. Treaties are negotiated in six stages; the first treaty under it, Tsawwassen, took effect in 2009.",
        """(?:the )?tsawwassen (?:first nation )?(?:final agreement|treaty)""" to "The first treaty reached under the BC treaty process and the first urban treaty in BC, in effect since April 3, 2009. The Tsawwassen First Nation in Delta owns about 724 hectares of land and governs itself outside the Indian Act.",
        """(?:the )?maa-?nulth (?:first nations )?(?:final agreement|treaty)""" to "A modern treaty in effect since April 1, 2011 with five Nuu-chah-nulth First Nations on the west coast of Vancouver Island: Huu-ay-aht, Ka:'yu:'k't'h'/Che:k'tles7et'h', Toquaht, Uchucklesaht and Yuułuʔiłʔatḥ.",
        """(?:the )?guerin (?:case|decision)|guerin v\.? (?:the )?(?:queen|r)""" to "Guerin v The Queen (1984): the federal government leased Musqueam reserve land to a golf club on worse terms than the band had agreed to. The Supreme Court ruled the Crown has a fiduciary duty, a duty of trust, to First Nations when dealing with their land.",
        """(?:the )?haida nation (?:case|decision)|haida nation v\.? british columbia""" to "Haida Nation v British Columbia (2004): BC let a logging licence on Haida Gwaii pass to a new company without asking the Haida. The Supreme Court ruled the Crown has a duty to consult, and where needed accommodate, First Nations before decisions that could affect rights they claim, even before those rights are proven.",
        """(?:the )?van der peet (?:case|decision)|r\.? v\.? van der peet""" to "R v Van der Peet (1996): Dorothy Van der Peet of the Stó:lō Nation was charged for selling ten salmon. The Supreme Court ruled that an Aboriginal right must be a practice integral to the distinctive culture of the group before contact, and that selling fish did not meet the test.",
        """free,? prior and informed consent|fpic""" to "The principle in the UN Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples that Indigenous peoples must be consulted in good faith and give their consent, freely, before a decision and with full information, to projects and laws that affect their lands and rights.",
        """(?:the )?royal proclamation(?: of 1763)?""" to "The 1763 proclamation by King George III after Britain took New France. It reserved lands west of the colonies for Indigenous nations and said only the Crown could acquire their land, by agreement, which became the basis for treaty-making. It is called the Indian Magna Carta.",
        """(?:the )?wet'?suwet'?en (?:pipeline )?(?:dispute|crisis|conflict|protests?|standoff)|(?:the )?coastal gaslink (?:pipeline )?(?:dispute|conflict)?""" to "The conflict over the Coastal GasLink natural gas pipeline across Wet'suwet'en territory in northern BC. The elected band councils signed agreements, but the hereditary chiefs opposed it. RCMP enforcement of injunctions in 2019 and February 2020 set off rail blockades in solidarity across Canada.",
        """(?:the )?gustafsen lake (?:standoff|crisis|siege)""" to "A month-long armed standoff from August to September 1995 near 100 Mile House between RCMP and Secwépemc sun dancers and supporters who refused to leave a sacred sun dance site on a private ranch, saying the land was unceded.",
        """(?:the )?haida gwaii (?:agreement|reconciliation|title agreement)|(?:the )?rising tide (?:haida )?(?:title )?agreement|kunst'?aa guu""" to "Agreements between the Haida Nation and BC: the 2009 Kunst'aa Guu-Kunst'aayah Reconciliation Protocol on sharing decisions about land, and the 2024 Rising Tide agreement in which BC recognized Haida Aboriginal title to all of Haida Gwaii, a first in Canada.",
        """(?:the )?great bear rainforest(?: agreements?)?""" to "The 6.4 million hectare temperate rainforest on BC's central and north coast. Agreements finalized in 2016 between BC, 26 First Nations, environmental groups and forest companies protect 85 percent of it from industrial logging, with First Nations sharing in management.",
        """(?:the )?royal commission on aboriginal peoples|rcap""" to "The federal royal commission set up in 1991 after the Oka crisis, which reported in 1996 with 440 recommendations, calling for a renewed nation-to-nation relationship and Indigenous self-government. Most were not acted on.",
        """(?:the )?(?:indian )?residential schools? settlement agreement|irssa""" to "The 2006 agreement, in effect in 2007, between survivors, the federal government and the churches, the largest class action settlement in Canadian history. It paid a Common Experience Payment to every survivor, set up a process for abuse claims and created the Truth and Reconciliation Commission.",
        """(?:a )?two[- ]spirit(?: people| person)?""" to "A term adopted in 1990 by Indigenous people for those who carry both masculine and feminine spirits, or whose gender or sexuality falls outside Western categories, reclaiming roles many nations honoured before colonization.",
        """(?:a )?medicine wheel""" to "A teaching symbol used by many First Nations, especially on the Plains, of a circle divided into four parts for the four directions, which can stand for the seasons, stages of life, and the physical, mental, emotional and spiritual sides of a person, all in balance.",
        """smudging|(?:a )?smudge ceremony""" to "A ceremony of burning sacred plants such as sage, sweetgrass, cedar or tobacco and drawing the smoke over oneself to cleanse the mind, body and spirit, often before a gathering or prayer. Practices differ from nation to nation.",
        """(?:a )?sweat lodges?""" to "A ceremony held in a low, dome-shaped lodge, where water poured on heated stones fills it with steam for prayer, healing and purification, led by a knowledge keeper.",
        """nunavut""" to "Canada's largest and newest territory, created on April 1, 1999 out of the eastern Northwest Territories under the Nunavut Land Claims Agreement. About 85 percent of its people are Inuit, and Inuktitut is an official language.",
        """(?:the )?jordan'?s principle""" to "The rule that First Nations children get the health and social services they need without delay, with governments sorting out who pays afterward. It is named for Jordan River Anderson of Norway House Cree Nation, who died in hospital in 2005 at age five while Manitoba and Ottawa argued over paying for his care at home. The House of Commons adopted it in 2007.",
        """(?:a )?(?:land|territorial|territory) acknowledge?ments?""" to "A statement at the start of an event recognizing the Indigenous peoples whose traditional territory it takes place on, such as \"We are on the unceded territory of the Musqueam, Squamish and Tsleil-Waututh Nations.\"",
        """(?:the )?nuu-?chah-?nulth(?: nations?| peoples?| tribal council)?""" to "The 14 First Nations of the west coast of Vancouver Island, from Port Renfrew to Brooks Peninsula, speaking a Wakashan language. They are known as whalers and seafarers, and the Maa-nulth treaty covers five of them.",
        """(?:the )?kwakwaka'?wakw(?: peoples?| nations?)?|(?:the )?kwakiutl""" to "The Kwak'wala-speaking peoples of northern Vancouver Island and the nearby mainland coast, such as at Alert Bay, Fort Rupert and Kingcome. They are renowned for the potlatch, masks and carving; the U'mista Cultural Centre holds regalia returned after the potlatch ban seizures.",
        """(?:the )?secw(?:e|é)pemc(?: nation| people)?|(?:the )?shuswap(?: nation| people)?""" to "The Secwépemc (Shuswap), an Interior Salish nation of the southern Interior around Kamloops and the Shuswap lakes, with 17 bands, speaking Secwepemctsín.",
        """(?:the )?syilx(?: okanagan)?(?: nation| people)?|(?:the )?okanagan nation""" to "The Syilx Okanagan Nation, Interior Salish people of the Okanagan Valley in BC and Washington State, speaking nsyilxcən.",
        """(?:the )?st(?:o|ó):?l(?:o|ō)(?: nation| people)?""" to "The Stó:lō, \"People of the River\", Coast Salish nations of the lower Fraser River and Fraser Valley, speaking Halq'eméylem.",
        """(?:the )?musqueam(?: indian band| nation| people)?|xʷməθkʷəy̓əm""" to "The xʷməθkʷəy̓əm (Musqueam), a Coast Salish nation at the mouth of the Fraser River in what is now Vancouver, speaking hən̓q̓əmin̓əm̓. They have lived there for thousands of years, and their cases, Guerin and Sparrow, shaped Aboriginal law.",
        """(?:a )?pit ?houses?|(?:a )?kekulis?|(?:a )?quiggly holes?""" to "A winter house of Interior peoples, dug about a metre or two into the ground and roofed with poles, bark and earth, entered by a ladder through the smoke hole. It stayed warm through cold Interior winters.",
        """(?:a )?button blankets?""" to "A ceremonial robe of Northwest Coast peoples, usually dark blue or black wool with a red border, decorated with the owner's crest in mother-of-pearl or shell buttons, worn at potlatches and other ceremonies.",
        """(?:a )?dugout canoes?""" to "A canoe carved from a single western red cedar log, hollowed out and widened by steaming with hot water and stones. Northwest Coast canoes ranged from small river canoes to ocean-going canoes over 15 metres long.",
        """(?:a )?chilkat blankets?|(?:a )?chilkat robes?""" to "A finely woven ceremonial robe made of mountain goat wool and cedar bark, with crest designs, first woven by the Tsimshian and Tlingit and prized all along the north coast. One can take a weaver a year or more.",
        """(?:a )?sacred sites?""" to "A place of spiritual importance to a people, used for ceremony, prayer, burial or vision quests, or tied to stories of creation. Many are threatened by development and are protected by First Nations' own laws and, increasingly, by provincial agreements.",
        """(?:the )?hudson'?s bay company|(?:the )?hbc""" to "The British fur-trading company chartered in 1670, which ran trade across much of what is now Canada. In BC it built posts like Fort Langley (1827) and Fort Victoria (1843), and its chief factor James Douglas became the first governor of the colonies.",
        """(?:the )?chilcotin war|(?:the )?tsilhqot'?in war""" to "In 1864 Tsilhqot'in warriors killed a road crew building Alfred Waddington's road from Bute Inlet into their territory without consent, amid smallpox and threats. Five Tsilhqot'in chiefs were hanged at Quesnel in 1864 and one more in 1865. BC exonerated them in 2014 and Canada in 2018.",
        """(?:the )?cariboo gold rush""" to "The gold rush of the early 1860s, peaking in 1862, centred on Barkerville in the Cariboo. Thousands of miners and the Cariboo Road pushed onto First Nations land without treaties.",
        """(?:the )?(?:bc |british columbia )assembly of first nations|(?:the )?bcafn""" to "The BC regional arm of the Assembly of First Nations, representing the chiefs of BC's First Nations, led by an elected Regional Chief.",
        """(?:the )?union of (?:bc|british columbia) indian chiefs|(?:the )?ubcic""" to "The political organization of First Nations in BC formed in 1969 to fight the federal White Paper, and since then a leading voice for Aboriginal title and rights.",
        """(?:the )?first nations summit""" to "An organization of First Nations in BC that speaks for those in treaty negotiations, and helped create the BC Treaty Commission in 1992 with Canada and BC.",
        """(?:the )?assembly of first nations|(?:the )?afn""" to "The national organization of First Nations chiefs in Canada, formed in 1982 from the National Indian Brotherhood, led by an elected National Chief.",
        """frank calder""" to "Frank Calder (1915 to 2006), Nisga'a leader, the first First Nations person elected to a Canadian legislature (BC, 1949) and the first to serve in a cabinet (1972). He led the Calder case, which in 1973 recognized that Aboriginal title exists in law.",
        """(?:grand chief )?george manuel""" to "George Manuel (1921 to 1989), Secwépemc leader from Neskonlith, president of the National Indian Brotherhood (1970 to 1976) and the Union of BC Indian Chiefs, founder of the World Council of Indigenous Peoples, and author of The Fourth World (1974).",
        """bill reid""" to "Bill Reid (1920 to 1998), Haida artist and carver who helped revive Northwest Coast art. His works include The Spirit of Haida Gwaii (The Jade Canoe) at Vancouver airport and The Raven and the First Men at the UBC Museum of Anthropology.",
        """(?:chief )?dan george""" to "Chief Dan George (1899 to 1981), chief of the Tsleil-Waututh Nation on Burrard Inlet, actor nominated for an Oscar for Little Big Man (1970), and author of the Lament for Confederation, spoken at Canada's centennial in 1967.",
        """jody wilson-raybould|puglaas""" to "Jody Wilson-Raybould (Puglaas), of the We Wai Kai Nation (Kwakwaka'wakw), former BC Regional Chief of the Assembly of First Nations and the first Indigenous person to serve as Canada's Minister of Justice and Attorney General, from 2015 to 2019.",
        """(?:the )?indian residential school survivors society|(?:the )?irsss""" to "A BC organization founded in 1994 that supports residential school survivors and their families with counselling, cultural support, a crisis line and help with healing.",
        """(?:the )?first peoples'? cultural council|(?:the )?fpcc""" to "A BC Crown corporation, founded in 1990 and run by First Nations, that supports the revitalization of First Nations languages, arts and heritage in BC, including the FirstVoices language site and language nests.",
        """(?:indigenous )?language revitali[sz]ation""" to "Bringing an endangered language back into daily use by teaching new speakers, through immersion schools, language nests, mentor-apprentice programs with fluent Elders, recordings and dictionaries such as FirstVoices.",
        """(?:a )?language nests?""" to "An immersion program where young children and their families spend time with fluent speakers, often Elders, hearing and using only the Indigenous language, modelled on the Māori kōhanga reo.",
        """(?:the )?indigenous languages act""" to "The 2019 federal law that recognizes Indigenous language rights as part of section 35 and commits to funding the revitalization of First Nations, Inuit and Métis languages, with a Commissioner of Indigenous Languages.",
        """reconciliation""" to "In Canada, reconciliation means building a respectful relationship between Indigenous and non-Indigenous peoples by facing the truth of the past, such as residential schools, and repairing the harm through action, as set out in the TRC's 94 Calls to Action.",
        """decoloni[sz]ation""" to "Undoing colonial systems and ways of thinking, and restoring Indigenous governance, laws, languages, land and knowledge, in education, government and everyday life.",
        """systemic racism|structural racism|institutional racism""" to "Racism built into the policies, practices and systems of institutions, such as justice, child welfare, policing or health care, that produce unequal results for racialized people even without individual intent.",
        """(?:the )?gladue (?:principle|decision|case|report)s?|r\.? v\.? gladue""" to "From R v Gladue (1999): under section 718.2(e) of the Criminal Code, judges sentencing an Indigenous person must consider the systemic and background factors, such as residential schools and poverty, that brought them to court, and look for alternatives to jail.",
        """(?:a )?healing circles?|(?:a )?talking circles?|(?:a )?sentencing circles?""" to "A gathering where people sit in a circle and speak in turn, often passing a talking stick or feather, to share, heal or settle a harm together. In justice, sentencing circles bring the offender, victim, Elders and community together.",
        """(?:an? )?oral (?:tradition|history|histories)""" to "Knowledge, history, law and stories passed down by speaking from one generation to the next, through storytelling, songs, names, dances and ceremonies such as the potlatch. Since Delgamuukw (1997), Canadian courts accept oral histories as evidence.",
        """(?:a )?clans?""" to "A group of families who share descent from a common ancestor, usually shown by a crest such as Raven, Eagle, Wolf or Killer Whale. In many north coast nations membership passes through the mother, and people marry outside their own clan.",
        """(?:the )?inuit""" to "The Indigenous peoples of the Arctic in Canada, Alaska, Greenland and Russia. In Canada about 70,000 Inuit live mostly in Inuit Nunangat: Nunavut, Nunavik in Quebec, Nunatsiavut in Labrador and the Inuvialuit region of the Northwest Territories. Most speak Inuktut.",
        """(?:the )?(?:maritime )?fur trade""" to "The trade of furs, beaver pelts inland and sea otter pelts on the coast, between First Nations and European traders from the late 1700s. It brought the Hudson's Bay Company and the North West Company to BC, new goods and wealth, and also disease and dependence.",
        """(?:the )?(?:truth and reconciliation commission|trc)(?: of canada)?""" to "The commission from 2008 to 2015, created by the residential schools settlement, that heard from more than 6,500 witnesses. Its 2015 report called the schools cultural genocide and made 94 Calls to Action.",
        """(?:the )?nisga'?a (?:final )?(?:agreement|treaty)""" to "BC's first modern treaty, in effect since May 11, 2000, giving the Nisga'a Nation in the Nass Valley ownership of about 2,000 square kilometres, self-government and rights to fish and resources, replacing the Indian Act.",
        """(?:the )?calder (?:case|decision)|calder v\.? (?:british columbia|attorney general)""" to "Calder v British Columbia (1973): Frank Calder and the Nisga'a sued for recognition of their title. The Supreme Court split on whether it still existed, but six judges agreed Aboriginal title existed in law before colonization, which led Canada to start negotiating land claims.",
        """(?:the )?delgamuukw (?:case|decision)|delgamuukw v\.? british columbia""" to "Delgamuukw v British Columbia (1997): a case brought by Gitxsan and Wet'suwet'en hereditary chiefs. The Supreme Court ruled that Aboriginal title is a right to the land itself, that it was never extinguished in BC, and that oral histories must be accepted as evidence.",
    ).map { Triple(Regex("^(?:" + it.first + ")$"), it.second, it.first) }

    private fun out(body: String) = Answer.Text(heading = null, body = body, source = "Nimble First Peoples")

    fun answer(q: String): Answer? {
        for ((re, body) in facts) if (re.containsMatchIn(q)) return out(body)
        val t = q.replace(Regex("^(?:who|what) (?:was|were|is|are) "), "").replace(Regex(" in (?:bc|british columbia|canada)$"), "")
        return Terms.answer(t, terms, emptyList())?.let { out(it) }
    }
}
